    "q-107779": {
      "dom_id": "step-q107779",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=4$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{27}, & 0<x<3, \\\\ \\frac{9-x}{27}, & 3\\le x<9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2x}{27}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{27}(9-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{27}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{1}{27}(9{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{4}}{54}|}_{0}^{3}+{\\frac{1}{27}(3{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{2}-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
